  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nUpper level trough is rotating through the area today with much \ncooler 850mb temperatures advecting into the region. 850mb \ntemperatures fall to around +8C this afternoon, which is just cool \nenough to create a limited lake effect response under the upper \nlevel trough (lake temperatures are near +23C). Therefore, expect \nscattered to broken clouds, and a few isolated showers today. The \ncold front slides off to the south and east this morning, with \ndecreasing low level moisture as dew points fall into the 50s this \nafternoon. NW winds will be breezy, between 10-20 mph and high \ntemperatures only reach the mid-60s to mid-70s, except upper 70s in \nthe Wyoming Valley.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nHigh pressure begins building into the area from the west tonight. \nWinds gradually diminish, becoming light and variable toward \ndaybreak. Skies become mainly clear and areas of valley fog are \nlikely to form overnight. It will be cool and crisp, with lows \ndipping down into the mid-40s to mid-50s areawide. Thursday sees \nhigh pressure build overhead, with mainly sunny skies. Low humidity \ncontinues and high temperatures are seasonably mild in the 70s. NW \nwinds will be lighter, under 10 mph through the day. High pressure \nremains centered overhead Thursday night into Friday morning. This \nbrings idea radiational cooling conditions once again. Lows dip down \ninto the upper 40s to mid-50s with more valley fog expected.\n\nFriday and Saturday look spectacular, with a zonal flow overhead and \na gradual warming trend expected. Skies remain mainly sunny both \ndays. Dew points stay low in the 50s, making it feel very \ncomfortable outside. High temperatures will be in the upper 70s to \nmid-80s each day, with refreshing overnight lows in the 50s to low \n60s forecast.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nThe weather pattern will turn more active late in the weekend and \ninto early next week as a broad upper level trough digs into the \nNortheast US. A new ridge develops over the western Plains by Sunday \nevening, with a shortwave dropping into the amplifying trough over \nthe Northeast and Mid-Atlantic heading into Monday/Tuesday. Low \nlevel moisture and instability gradually increases Sunday, but \nespecially heading into Monday and Tuesday. PWATs reach 1.50 inches \nby Sunday evening, and 1.75 inches by Monday evening as surface dew \npoints also rise back into the 60s. Weak disturbances are expected \nto initiate scattered afternoon and evening thunderstorms each day. \nA deeper, sharper trough may push into the area by Tuesday, \nincreasing chances for showers and storms once again. The exact \ntiming and details remain uncertain in the guidance this far out in \ntime. High temperatures will be in the upper 70s to mid-80s Sunday, \ngradually dropping a few degrees early next week. Overnight lows \nlook to be seasonable in the upper 50s to low 60s.",
